1
00:00:00,05 --> 00:00:02,07
- [Instructor] State management refers to the management

2
00:00:02,07 --> 00:00:05,07
and manipulation of data within a web application.

3
00:00:05,07 --> 00:00:07,05
It is the process for managing the data

4
00:00:07,05 --> 00:00:10,00
in your React application with the implementation

5
00:00:10,00 --> 00:00:12,04
of a design pattern to synchronize the state

6
00:00:12,04 --> 00:00:15,05
throughout all the components of the application.

7
00:00:15,05 --> 00:00:17,04
In a typical React application,

8
00:00:17,04 --> 00:00:19,06
the data is stored in a state object.

9
00:00:19,06 --> 00:00:21,04
The state object represents the state

10
00:00:21,04 --> 00:00:24,00
of the application at any given time.

11
00:00:24,00 --> 00:00:26,03
The way it works is that the view subscribes

12
00:00:26,03 --> 00:00:27,07
to the state object.

13
00:00:27,07 --> 00:00:29,07
Whenever the view receives an interaction,

14
00:00:29,07 --> 00:00:31,02
and action is dispatched.

15
00:00:31,02 --> 00:00:33,06
Then the view updates the user interface

16
00:00:33,06 --> 00:00:36,08
on every state changes, and you can have multiple views

17
00:00:36,08 --> 00:00:39,01
that share the same state updates.

18
00:00:39,01 --> 00:00:41,08
Whenever there is a change in the state object,

19
00:00:41,08 --> 00:00:44,01
the state updates are shared and synchronized

20
00:00:44,01 --> 00:00:47,00
across all the components of the application.

21
00:00:47,00 --> 00:00:50,04
Implementing state management is not an easy process though.

22
00:00:50,04 --> 00:00:53,01
It requires time, lots of thinking, planning,

23
00:00:53,01 --> 00:00:54,03
and maintenance.

24
00:00:54,03 --> 00:00:56,04
So finding the right state management solution

25
00:00:56,04 --> 00:00:58,04
is a great deal for any development team

26
00:00:58,04 --> 00:01:01,08
to guarantee the success of your web projects.

27
00:01:01,08 --> 00:01:03,06
So which solution should I pick?

28
00:01:03,06 --> 00:01:04,08
As a React developer,

29
00:01:04,08 --> 00:01:07,05
your role is not to pick a solution blindly,

30
00:01:07,05 --> 00:01:08,08
or to stick to one choice

31
00:01:08,08 --> 00:01:11,07
even if you are an unconditional fan.

32
00:01:11,07 --> 00:01:14,01
You will need to think about which solution is best

33
00:01:14,01 --> 00:01:15,07
for your needs.

34
00:01:15,07 --> 00:01:18,05
Back in 2015, at the time when designs

35
00:01:18,05 --> 00:01:20,06
and architecture like Flux had become trendy,

36
00:01:20,06 --> 00:01:24,06
Dan Abramov once shared that "It is in React's spirit

37
00:01:24,06 --> 00:01:26,07
to constantly question the dogma.

38
00:01:26,07 --> 00:01:28,02
And we must be careful

39
00:01:28,02 --> 00:01:30,07
not to introduce a dogma of our own.

40
00:01:30,07 --> 00:01:33,03
As a community, we need to teach the beginners

41
00:01:33,03 --> 00:01:35,01
to keep their eyes wide open

42
00:01:35,01 --> 00:01:36,05
so they can recognize when something better

43
00:01:36,05 --> 00:01:38,07
than Flux comes along."

44
00:01:38,07 --> 00:01:40,09
So there is no need to implement state management

45
00:01:40,09 --> 00:01:43,09
by following one design pattern only.

46
00:01:43,09 --> 00:01:46,09
For example, while you've heard great benefits

47
00:01:46,09 --> 00:01:48,06
about the Redux Library,

48
00:01:48,06 --> 00:01:50,09
your small application might not need Redux.

49
00:01:50,09 --> 00:01:52,04
Don't get me wrong, though.

50
00:01:52,04 --> 00:01:55,01
Redux is by far a great library,

51
00:01:55,01 --> 00:01:56,09
generally preferred for managing the state

52
00:01:56,09 --> 00:01:59,09
in large web applications handling lots of data.

53
00:01:59,09 --> 00:02:01,09
It is just that Redux is known

54
00:02:01,09 --> 00:02:04,03
for requiring lots of boilerplate code,

55
00:02:04,03 --> 00:02:06,04
you need to add and create actions.

56
00:02:06,04 --> 00:02:09,02
It also introduces lots of complexity into your projects

57
00:02:09,02 --> 00:02:11,03
and it is good to note as well

58
00:02:11,03 --> 00:02:13,09
that the learning curve is not simple.

59
00:02:13,09 --> 00:02:17,09
Lucky for us developers, the React ecosystem is very rich.

60
00:02:17,09 --> 00:02:20,04
The great benefits is that React is highly flexible

61
00:02:20,04 --> 00:02:23,03
and can be used everywhere.

62
00:02:23,03 --> 00:02:25,03
Some popular state management solutions

63
00:02:25,03 --> 00:02:29,04
includes the React API with useState and useReducer.

64
00:02:29,04 --> 00:02:31,01
So no need to write a class anymore.

65
00:02:31,01 --> 00:02:33,06
The state can be managed within functional components

66
00:02:33,06 --> 00:02:36,01
that can hold and maintain their own states,

67
00:02:36,01 --> 00:02:38,04
thanks to the hooks which have been in the React library

68
00:02:38,04 --> 00:02:40,03
since 2018.

69
00:02:40,03 --> 00:02:44,05
Another built-in feature of the React API is the Context API

70
00:02:44,05 --> 00:02:47,03
that provides a way to create a global state

71
00:02:47,03 --> 00:02:49,02
and a single source of truth.

72
00:02:49,02 --> 00:02:51,03
In this course, we're going to see a few examples

73
00:02:51,03 --> 00:02:52,08
with the Context API.

74
00:02:52,08 --> 00:02:54,02
Then you have Redux,

75
00:02:54,02 --> 00:02:56,02
which is a popular state management library

76
00:02:56,02 --> 00:02:58,01
for the React applications.

77
00:02:58,01 --> 00:03:01,00
Redux provides a centralized store for an easy managements

78
00:03:01,00 --> 00:03:04,02
of complex states across multiple components.

79
00:03:04,02 --> 00:03:06,01
Then you have other solutions like MobX

80
00:03:06,01 --> 00:03:08,03
that works with reactive programming

81
00:03:08,03 --> 00:03:10,08
and Vuex for view applications.

82
00:03:10,08 --> 00:03:12,03
And you have the new kids on the block,

83
00:03:12,03 --> 00:03:14,03
Recoil and Zustand.

84
00:03:14,03 --> 00:03:16,05
Lightweight with anatomic approach,

85
00:03:16,05 --> 00:03:18,09
both are designed to be simple, flexible

86
00:03:18,09 --> 00:03:20,00
and easy to use

87
00:03:20,00 --> 00:03:23,04
with a syntax very similar to React without the complexity

88
00:03:23,04 --> 00:03:26,01
of traditional state management libraries.

89
00:03:26,01 --> 00:03:28,05
And they both provide a simple way to manage the state

90
00:03:28,05 --> 00:03:32,01
without the need for complex patterns or libraries.

91
00:03:32,01 --> 00:03:34,06
Overall, effective state management leads

92
00:03:34,06 --> 00:03:37,01
to easy data sharing and communication,

93
00:03:37,01 --> 00:03:39,02
easy software testing and debugging,

94
00:03:39,02 --> 00:03:41,07
and a better performance in optimization.

95
00:03:41,07 --> 00:03:44,08
And the benefits are visible both from a technical

96
00:03:44,08 --> 00:03:48,00
and a business perspective with a register development time

97
00:03:48,00 --> 00:03:51,01
and cost, with an improved cut organization,

98
00:03:51,01 --> 00:03:54,00
a better reusability and scalability.

